    "message": {
     "content": "### 1. The Verdict: Is This a Good Offer?\n\n**Yes, this is a strong initial offer for an L6 PM, but you should not accept it as-is.** \n\nBecause you were passively recruited, are happy in your current role at Meta, and carry zero risk if discussions fall through, **you have maximum leverage.** Google rarely gives its best numbers in the first pass, and leaving a base salary cut and a missing sign-on bonus unaddressed would leave substantial compensation on the table.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Financial Breakdown: Google vs. Meta\n\nA common misconception is treating Google\u2019s $1,080,000 RSU grant as an even 25% ($270,000) per year. Google's standard new-hire equity vests on a **front-loaded schedule (33% / 33% / 22% / 12%)**:\n\n| Component | Current Meta Comp | Google Year 1 & 2 (Per Year) | Google Year 3 | Google Year 4 |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $240,000 | $235,000 | $235,000 | $235,000 |\n| **Bonus Target** | $36,000 (15%) | $47,000 (20%) | $47,000 (20%) | $47,000 (20%) |\n| **RSU Vesting** | ~$200,000\u2013$250,000 | **$356,400** (33%) | **$237,600** (22%) | **$129,600** (12%) |\n| **Sign-On** | \u2014 | $0 | \u2014 | \u2014 |\n| **Total Comp (TC)**| **~$476,000 \u2013 $526,000** | **$638,400** | **$519,600** *(+ ref)* | **$411,600** *(+ ref)* |\n\n#### Key Takeaways:\n- **Years 1 & 2:** At **~$638k TC**, you receive an immediate ~$110k\u2013$160k annual pay bump over Meta.\n- **The \"Cliff\" in Years 3 & 4:** Total comp drops sharply because of the front-loaded grant. While annual refreshers (which vest 25%/yr over 4 years) stack to bridge this gap, they are discretionary and performance-dependent.\n- **Base Salary Deficit:** You would be taking a **$5,000 base pay cut** ($240k $\\rightarrow$ $235k), despite the role\u2019s posted ceiling reaching **$271,000**.\n\n---\n\n### 3. What to Negotiate (Specific Ask)\n\nGoogle compensation teams expect counter-offers at L6. You should anchor your counter on three clear components:\n\n1. **Base Salary \u2014 Ask for $255,000 (Offer: $235,000)**\n   - *Rationale:* You should not take a base salary reduction when moving laterally between Tier-1 tech firms. $255k sits comfortably within Google's $183k\u2013$271k band.\n2. **Sign-On Bonus \u2014 Ask for $75,000 \u2013 $100,000 (Offer: $0)**\n   - *Rationale:* Sign-on bonuses are Google\u2019s primary discretionary lever to close lateral hires from Meta/Apple/Netflix. It offsets the risk of walking away from your established reputation and upcoming unvested Meta equity refreshes.\n3. **Equity (GSUs) \u2014 Ask for $1,250,000 over 4 years (Offer: $1,080,000)**\n   - *Rationale:* Bumps Year 1 & 2 vesting from $356k to **$412.5k/year**, ensuring meaningful upside and cushioning the back-half taper.\n\n---\n\n### 4. How to Execute the Counter\n\nReach out to the recruiter via phone or email with a polite, collaborative, but firm message. Emphasize that you are enthusiastic about the team and scope, but need the financial picture aligned since you weren't actively looking.\n\n#### Counter Script / Email Template:\n\n> *\"Hi [Recruiter Name],*\n>\n> *Thank you again for putting this offer together. I\u2019ve enjoyed meeting the team, and I\u2019m genuinely excited about the scope and product challenges we discussed.*\n>\n> *Because I wasn't actively looking to leave Meta and am happy with my trajectory here, I spent time reviewing the economics. To make this move an easy decision, there are a few gaps we need to close:*\n>\n> 1. * **Base Salary:** My current base at Meta is $240,000. I'd like to avoid taking a step back on fixed cash. Given the posted range goes up to $271,000, I\u2019m targeting **$255,000**.*\n> 2. * **Sign-on Bonus:** Walking away from my established performance track and upcoming Meta refresh cycle represents significant unvested upside. A sign-on bonus of **$80,000** would bridge that gap.*\n> 3. * **Equity:** To align long-term incentives with the scope of this L6 role, I\u2019m hoping we can increase the initial grant to **$1,250,000**.*\n>\n> *If we can get the package to this level, I\u2019m ready to sign and get started with the team. How much flexibility does the compensation committee have on these levers?\"*\n\n---\n\n### 5. Final Recommendation\n\n- **Negotiate:** Send the counter above. There is virtually zero risk of Google rescinding an L6 PM offer over standard, polite negotiation.\n- **Expected Outcome:** Google will likely meet you in the middle\u2014expect base pushed to ~$245k\u2013$250k, a $40k\u2013$60k sign-on bonus, and an equity bump to ~$1.15M\u2013$1.2M.\n- **Decision Rule:** If they improve the package (or at least provide a sign-on bonus and match your base), take the offer if you want broader product diversity/brand prestige. If they refuse to move at all, you can comfortably stay at Meta knowing your baseline compensation and career progression are secure.",
